{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem explicitly requires identifying what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_001's mechanism argues that the checklist caps accuracy at the designer's level, but never provides any evidentiary criterion for separating a panel that arrives at correct answers through genuine reliability from one that merely complies with checklist steps and coincidentally tracks the right answer. The entire target omits the second half of the problem's mandate.","target":"3d038dc9f9a7966d145120e5256b9fe0ae179f780843aa400789ed37d1feb4e9"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"b4312cc0d5de3adae57ccab59da9265576f627ee6639d22fc81209faad14fa4c"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 frames the panel's reliability as an 'illusion of precision'—being highly reliable at solving the checklist version of the problem—but never specifies what evidence within a single evaluation would separate this illusion from a genuine reliability gain. The problem demands a criterion for distinguishing genuine gain from mere compliance that happens to track the right answer; SRC_003 merely asserts the panel's success is illusory without supplying the discriminating evidence the problem requires.","target":"d72e19fd3ef6ef6aa6e4771da26a89f8223b876ab6d34399387b585033d74c00"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 claims reliability is a function of error-type symmetry and that experts and panels fail in opposite directions, but it never addresses the problem's core requirement: specifying what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. The mechanism describes failure modes but provides no criterion for separating authentic reliability from coincidental compliance.","target":"e663645ac71833e09775155ebd03b54b0f6fe50f7c2f1da7384ca3469d6efb6a"}]}